to be an artist is to be suspicious of  my  and yes is probably more weary than most  she was called the grandmother of French  new-wave  when she was thirty how she must have  rolled her eyes and I imagine she is  doing that at this very moment  throughout her career she has held up to  the viewer the beauty and nobility of  everyday life not the epic canvass of  survival on a battlefield but the  dignity and heroism of the person next  to us their struggles their memories and  their value she would be the last to  claim any special status but we honor  her tonight because she does occupy a  special space she made her first film in  1954 63 years ago when cinema was still  a young art she was 25 years old  self-taught had never written or even  read a script she told herself she only  intended to direct that one film yet he  or she is today still a pioneer still  making beautiful films with a touch as  deft and original as when she first  picked up the camera female director is  another label she might resist she is of  course first and foremost an artist a  creative force a brilliant mind and  inquiring spirit a communicator when she  began she did not just make films no  woman had ever made they were films no  one was making but yet it is also true  that she was one of only a handful of  small of female directors in the world  at the time and we all know what that  represents setting out where there are  no precedents to follow overcoming  skeptics  and Prejudice facing all the extra  hurdles in the path of a working mother  that women are to still have to fight to  be free to pursue their craft on an  equal footing today is something that I  imagine she never expected so we need to  draw strength from artists like Enya's  those women who went first who took that  first step  showed the way for all of us who saw a  little further than others around them  who said I will speak I will write I  will go there that way is for me who  broke convention and freed the  co